opencl: Deliver event callbacks from a dispatcher thread, and flush for real.

ElementalWarrior's OpenCL bridge delivers clSetEventCallback callbacks by
polling, as a side effect of later OpenCL calls (base 0003, retimed by the
set's 0012), and its 0030 turns clFlush into a no-op. Affinity registers a
completion callback while starting up and then waits for it without making
another OpenCL call, so the callback never ran -- and with clFlush gone the
command it waited on was not necessarily submitted at all. Affinity hung at
startup whenever OpenCL was enabled on a real GPU, which is why the
launchers disable opencl.dll.

Register callbacks with the host driver. The driver fires them on threads
of its own, which cannot run PE code, so the unix side takes a reference to
the event and queues it, and one Wine thread, started on first use, calls
the application's callback and releases the event, the way a Windows driver
calls it from a thread of its own. Callbacks run one at a time in the order
the driver fired them. clFlush submits the queue again. clSVMAlloc and
clSVMFree, which the bridge did not export, are added.

With this, Affinity 3.3 starts with OpenCL on an Intel Arc A750 (2026-10-02);
filter-heavy exports are ~30% faster, interactive work unchanged. opencl=d
stays the default in the launchers until this is in a release.

